reading: shared-prefix chars are converted at the measured 2.59 ch/tok value density, which
is an UPPER bound on the saving because a character removed from a value is not always worth
1/2.59 of a token in context. The length digits are priced exactly. NET is therefore
optimistic, and the contract tax for describing front coding (the measured break-even for a
new contract clause is ~20-25 tokens) is NOT subtracted. A positive NET here is necessary
but not sufficient; a negative one closes the lever outright.
